| 181 | the preparser reads a backtick escape in a grammar production as an opening quote wanting a closing `'` and rejects the api; the macro language's own special characters, APL's `+` and `*` among them, need that escape | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 1 | siblings: 211. `Unmatched delimiter "api".` and ``Unmatched delimiters "`" and "end".`` `PreCompilation.rats:115` puts the backtick in `Opening`; `PreParserState.java:256` pairs it only with `'` or `’`. Inside `⦇ ⦈` it also breaks their pairing. Workaround: an APL glyph above the escape: the preparser cannot tokenise it, its token loop (`PreCompilation.rats:56`, no end-of-file anchor) stops, and `report()` never runs; `explorations/apl/base/AplSyntax.fsi` keeps `⌿ ⍬ ⍎` above its escaped `+`. Probes `explorations/apl/v1-probes/p08a_g.fsi` (fails), `p08b_g.fsi` (works), `p08d_g.fsi`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 181 |
| 183 | an **undelimited** host `Expr` gap swallows any following terminal the host knows as an operator (`≡`, `⋄`, `∘`), because a gap is greedy and Rats never re-enters a nonterminal for a shorter match | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 1 | siblings: 235. `esc2⦇ v ≡ m ⦈` makes the expander unparsable (`Variable esc2 is not defined`); `apl⦇ ⍎v ≡ 1 4⍴⍎v ⦈` parsed as `aplDy("rho", v EQV (1 4), v)`. In `y⦇ a:Expr ⋄ b:Expr ⦈` (row 284) the first gap takes `a ⋄ b` as the host's `DIAMOND` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Literal.rats:428`), `∘` (`RING`, `:403-405`) likewise, leaving a call of an undefined `y` (`Variable y4 is not defined.`). Workarounds: bracket the gap (`⍎(v)`), or follow it with a sub-language nonterminal or a terminal in no host operator table (`⍎v ⍴ ⍎m` parses). Probes `explorations/apl/v1-probes/p03b_escape.fss`, `probes-4b/y01_named.fss`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 183 |
| 196 | a template cannot expand to a **declaration** with a gap left of `=`: the template parser reads the left of `=` and of `:=` with its `AssignLeft` production, where a gap's text arrives null | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 2 | siblings: 186. `MacroError: Could not parse 'do <!@#$%^&*<Id t >*&^%$#@!> = ( <!@#$%^&*<Expr r >*&^%$#@!> ); …` / `Caused by: java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Parameter 'text' to the IdOrOp constructor was null` at `TemplateParser3.pNoNewlineExpr$AssignLeft`, wrapped by `parseTemplate` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/syntax_abstractions/phases/TemplateParser.java:100-121`). With row 186, no gap may stand left of `=` or `:=`, so APL's `←` is a workspace call (row 195), not a Fortress binding. Probe `explorations/apl/v1-probes/p16_decl.fss`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 196 |
| 213 | a **static error inside an expansion is reported against the grammar api**, at its line 1, not at the use site that caused it | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | `Specification/advanced/domain-specific-languages.tex`, "Support for Domain-Specific Languages" | none | apl rung 2b | Every undefined name gets a span in the grammar, not the user's file: `explorations/apl/rung-4/u20_free.fss` with `g`'s binding removed gives `Variable g is not defined.` at `AplSyntax.fsi:1:2`, killing the whole component (the book's Ex 14). A missing rank overload reached through an expansion is a unification error at `AplSyntax.fsi:1:1-107` (`Cannot unify Float … with Vector[\RR64,u\]`), not a dispatch failure at the use site. Run as `explorations/apl/rung-4/REPORT.md`, "How to run", says. The paper disagrees: §1 has a syntax error at a use site refer to the unexpanded use site; a name error is no syntax error, but its span is the definition site. full text: history, row 213 |
| 229 | a use of an expander that **matches no production** is not reported as a syntax error: the bracketed text is left unparsed and the keyword before it is read as an ordinary identifier, `Variable apl is not defined` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | `Specification/advanced/domain-specific-languages.tex`, "Support for Domain-Specific Languages" | `explorations/apl/rung-2/s07_chain.fss` | apl rung 2 | `Variable apl is not defined.` at line 8, columns 51 to 59, for `apl⦇ a ← b ← 1 2 ⋄ a ⦈`: the message names neither the sub-language nor the line that is wrong (row 213 misplaces the span; this one has no syntax error at all). A *partial* match does say `Syntax Error` at the right column (`explorations/apl/rung-2/Rung2.out.0`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5). Run as `explorations/apl/rung-2/REPORT.md`, "How to run", says, with `explorations/apl/base` on the source path. The paper the chapter defers to disagrees: §1 requires syntax errors at use sites to refer to the unexpanded program at use sites. full text: history, row 229 |
| 235 | a gap is greedy **inside a delimited rule** as well: `( a:AplE SPACE , SPACE b:AplE ) ⍴ r` never matches once the grammar also has a catenate rule, because the first gap parses `a , b` as one catenation and PEG does not retry it shorter | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | `explorations/apl/rung-3/t02_gram.fss` | apl rung 3 | siblings: 183, 287. The closing delimiter is no barrier the gap can see. `explorations/apl/rung-3/T02Syn.fsi:30` (`TExp` gaps) stands above `:32` (`TAtom` gaps), and the reproducer printed `TA gaps: 5 5 5`, the atom rule firing. Run as `explorations/apl/rung-3/REPORT.md`, "How to run", says, with `explorations/apl/base` on the source path. Workaround: atom gaps; `explorations/apl/base/AplSyntax.fsi:212` writes `( SPACE a:AplAtom SPACE , SPACE b:AplAtom SPACE ) SPACE ⍴ SPACE r:AplE`, enough for `(n,NE)⍴x`, the microGPT spelling, but not for `(2+1,3)⍴x`. full text: history, row 235 |
| 236 | a rule whose left argument is **parenthesised** must stand **above** the plain rule for the same glyph: below it, `AplAtom`'s own `( AplE )` consumes the parenthesis first and the specialised rule is never reached | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 3 | siblings: 287. Not a syntax error but a wrong parse that reaches the library: `(2,3)` becomes a vector and `(2,3)⍴5` a plain dyadic `⍴`, so it surfaces as `Failed to find any matching overload, args = (__DefaultVector[\RR64,2\],5.0:RR64)` for `aplReshapeV` against the grammar api (row 230's shape). Reproduce: `explorations/apl/rung-3/t02_gram.fss` with the two `(a,b)⍴` rules of `explorations/apl/rung-3/T02Syn.fsi:30-33` moved below `:34`, the plain `l:TAtom SPACE ⍴` rule (output `t02_gram.out.5`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5). Workaround, and the whole fix: rule order; `explorations/apl/base/AplSyntax.fsi:212` stands above the numeral rules at `:220-221`. full text: history, row 236 |
| 239 | a **use of the expander cannot be an operand of a juxtaposition**: `("" apl⦇ 5 ⦈)` is `Variable apl is not defined`, while the same use parses as a function argument, as the right-hand side of a binding, and as an argument inside a juxtaposition | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 3 | siblings: 229, 287. `explorations/apl/rung-3/t07_juxt.fss` runs the three positions that work; its case (d), `println(("five = " apl⦇ 5 ⦈))`, commented out at `t07_juxt.fss:22`, gave `Variable apl is not defined.` when live (`t07_juxt.out.0`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5). Run as `explorations/apl/rung-3/REPORT.md`, "How to run", says. The newline-terminated form cannot stand in an argument list at all (row 209); the bracketed form can, and fails only in a juxtaposition. The diagnostic is row 229's and does not say what is wrong. Workarounds: bind the use first, wrap it in a call, or parenthesise the use itself. full text: history, row 239 |
| 250 | **`\` and `.` are plain items of a production and refuse the backtick escape**: neither is one of the macro language's SpecialChars, so `` `\ `` and `` `. `` are Syntax Errors where the bare characters parse and match | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 5 | `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Syntax.rats:395-398` (`SpecialChars = space / breakline / colon / questionmark / hash / plus / star / opensquare / closesquare / pling / bar / underscore / "{" / "}"`) and `:400` (`EscapedSpecialChars`). `explorations/apl/rung-5/v02_term.fss` runs the bare spellings; with `\` escaped in `explorations/apl/rung-5/V02Syn.fsi:78` it gave `V02Syn.fsi:78:13: Syntax Error`, and with `.` escaped in `:91`, `V02Syn.fsi:91:26` (`v02_term.out.0`, `.out.1`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5). Workaround: write `+\`, `∘.×` and `+.×` with the bare character; row 181's glyph-above rule does not apply. `¨ ⍀ ⍣` and `⌷` work as plain items. full text: history, row 250 |
| 287 | **rule order decides only inside an ordinary nonterminal**: alternatives of one macro bracket name backtrack over the closing bracket, but inside a nonterminal PEG commits to the first alternative that succeeds, so the folded reduction `+/l×r` must stand above the plain `+/v` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 4b | siblings: 235, 236. The closing `⦈` is part of each bracket alternative, so a shorter one above a longer one still lets the longer match; inside a nonterminal the caller cannot ask for another parse (row 235's greed). Workaround: put the pair in a nonterminal, the folded rule first. A probe of this must not juxtapose a bare macro bracket as an argument: that use fails on its own (row 239, `Variable s6 is not defined.`). Probe `explorations/apl/probes-4b/y06_fold.fss`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5; its record is `explorations/apl/probes-4b/PROBES.md` (y06). full text: history, row 287 |
